{{also|UG|Ug|.ug}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ʌɡ/}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|ugge}}, from {{der|en|non|uggr||fear, apprehension, dread}}, related to {{cog|non|ógn||terror, threat, dispute}} and {{m|non|agi||terror, strife, fear, punishment}}. More at {{l|en|awe}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|ugg}}, {{l|en|oug}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Ireland|and|Scotland|obsolete}} A feeling of [[revulsion]], or [[disgust]].
#: {{usex|en|He took an '''ug''' at's meht.}}<!-- as used in The English Dialect Dictionary‎ -->
# {{lb|en|Ireland|and|Scotland|obsolete}} An object of [[disgust]].
#: {{usex|en|What an '''ug''' ye've myed yorsel laddie.}}<!-- as used in The English Dialect Dictionary‎ -->
# {{lb|en|Irelend|and|Scotland|obsolete}} [[aversion]] matter.
# {{lb|en|Northumbria}} A [[surfeit]].

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|fear; horror}} {{l|en|dread}}, {{l|en|fright}}; see also [[Thesaurus:fear]]
* {{sense|terror}} {{l|en|threat}}, {{l|en|apprehension}}, {{l|en|foreboding}}
* {{sense|dread}} {{l|en|disquietude}}
* {{sense|trepidation}} {{l|en|shock}}, {{l|en|intimidation}}
* {{sense|scapegrace}} {{l|en|radical}}, {{l|en|firebrand}}

=====Derived from old norse language=====
* {{l|en|ugga}}
* {{l|en|uggr}}
* {{l|en|uggen}}

